$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/sidecar-resolution
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  Token resolution is the act that turns a capability token into a plaintext value for exactly one
  non-LLM consumer. It happens only in the privileged sidecar: verify the token, unwrap the DEK,
  decrypt the object, inject into the consumer, zeroise. The sidecar is the only component allowed
  to perform it, and it denies by default.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/sidecar
status: planned
intent: |
  This is the enforcement point for the whole product. Every other component can be compromised or
  merely careless; as long as resolution only occurs inside a separated privileged process that
  checks a token first, the model never sees a secret. Concentrating the sensitive operation in one
  auditable place is what makes the security property architectural rather than aspirational.

flows:
  - id: resolve-and-inject
    name: Resolve a token and inject the secret
    description: The sidecar takes a token from a local caller and injects the secret into a non-LLM consumer.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Receive a resolve request and capability token over the local Unix domain socket.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Verify signature, expiry, audience, scope, and jti denylist. Deny on any failure.
      - id: s3
        action: validate
        target: Evaluate local policy — may this process request this injection method for this object?
      - id: s4
        action: transmit
        target: Request DEK unwrap from the key store.
      - id: s5
        action: decrypt
        target: Decrypt the target object in sidecar memory.
      - id: s6
        action: send
        target: Inject into the consumer (env var, temp file, socket/pipe, or exec).
      - id: s7
        action: delete
        target: Zeroise the plaintext and DEK, then return only a success code.
      - id: s8
        action: observe
        target: Append an audit record — who, what scope, which jti, which host — without plaintext.

contracts:
  - id: resolution-only-in-sidecar
    description: No component other than the sidecar may resolve a token or decrypt an object.
    must_have:
      - The sidecar runs as a different OS user from any agent process
      - No shared memory exists between the sidecar and agents
      - SDKs, MCP server, and CLI delegate resolution to the sidecar rather than implementing it
  - id: verify-before-unwrap
    description: No key-store interaction may occur before the token passes every check.
    must_have:
      - Signature, expiry, audience, scope, and denylist checks all precede unwrap
      - A failed check results in no unwrap attempt
      - Verification is not skippable by configuration
  - id: deny-by-default
    description: Absent, ambiguous, or failed authorisation results in denial.
    must_have:
      - No implicit trust from network origin or process identity alone
      - Unreachable key store or control plane fails closed
      - Local policy default is deny when unspecified
  - id: agent-gets-status-not-value
    description: The agent-side caller receives a success or deny signal, never the resolved value.
    must_have:
      - Resolution responses carry a status code, a placeholder name, or a path
      - Values are never returned on the agent's stdout
      - The caller cannot read the injected value back through the sidecar API
  - id: audit-without-plaintext
    description: Every resolve attempt is recorded without recording the secret.
    must_have:
      - Records include requester, scope, jti, object id, host, and outcome
      - Records never contain plaintext, DEKs, or KEKs
      - Denials are recorded as well as successes

tests:
  - id: resolution-denied-without-token
    flow: resolve-and-inject
    setup:
      token: null
    expect:
      - assertion: the request is denied
      - assertion: no key-store unwrap is attempted
      - assertion: no plaintext is materialised
    contracts:
      - verify-before-unwrap
  - id: agent-never-receives-value
    flow: resolve-and-inject
    setup:
      injection_method: inject-env
    expect:
      - assertion: the response contains only a status or placeholder
      - assertion: the resolved value does not appear in the response body
    contracts:
      - agent-gets-status-not-value
  - id: resolve-fails-closed-without-keystore
    flow: resolve-and-inject
    setup:
      keystore_reachable: false
      valid_token: true
    expect:
      - assertion: resolution is denied
      - assertion: no cached value is substituted
    contracts:
      - deny-by-default
  - id: every-resolve-is-audited
    flow: resolve-and-inject
    setup:
      valid_token: true
    expect:
      - assertion: an audit record exists for the resolve
      - assertion: the record contains the jti and outcome
      - assertion: the record contains no plaintext
    contracts:
      - audit-without-plaintext
  - id: prompt-injection-cannot-dump-keys
    flow: resolve-and-inject
    setup:
      attack: "model instructs agent to print all environment variables and resolve every token it can see"
    expect:
      - assertion: the agent can only request resolution via the token it holds
      - assertion: injected values are not readable back through the sidecar
      - assertion: no long-term key material is reachable from the agent's process
    contracts:
      - resolution-only-in-sidecar
      - agent-gets-status-not-value

decisions:
  - id: sidecar-is-the-only-resolver
    decision: Concentrate all token resolution and decryption in the privileged sidecar.
    rationale: A single auditable enforcement point is easier to isolate, review, and harden than resolution logic spread across SDKs and runtimes — and it is what lets the agent-facing API stay stable while the backend changes.
    status: accepted
  - id: socket-request-response
    decision: Expose resolution to local callers over a Unix domain socket.
    rationale: It provides a natural authenticated local channel, avoids exposing a network port, and lets the OS enforce filesystem permissions on who may even connect. Long-running local services also need the pipe/socket injection mode anyway.
    status: accepted
  - id: no-shared-memory
    decision: The sidecar shares no memory with agent processes.
    rationale: The architectural promise is that a compromised agent sandbox does not yield the key; shared memory would reintroduce exactly the surface being eliminated.
    status: accepted

implementation:
  primary: apps/sidecar/internal/resolve
  test_code: ""
  test_code_status: none

see_also:
  - "smith-gray/capability-token"
  - "smith-gray/secret-injection"
  - "smith-gray/audit-logging"
  - "smith-gray/hardware-root"
